{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high vs realized prices, especially after the first week; apply a downward level shift (≈ -0.5 to -1.0 EUR early horizon, widening to ≈ -3 to -8 EUR by ~day 10–30 in these samples).",
    "Trend/mean-reversion mismatch: when the recent history is in a downtrend, the model reverts upward too quickly (predicts rebounds that don’t materialize). Reduce mean-reversion strength and allow declines to persist longer.",
    "Overshoot after short-term upticks: following 1–3 day bounces in history, the model extrapolates into multi-day rises, but outcomes typically resume falling/flat. Damp positive slope projections after brief rebounds.",
    "Horizon-dependent drift: forecast error grows with lead time (small near-term error, large long-horizon overestimation). Use a lead-time scaling correction where the downward adjustment increases with horizon.",
    "Volatility overstatement on the upside: model produces higher peaks and slower drawdowns than realized. Cap upside excursions relative to recent levels and accelerate drawdown dynamics after local highs."
  ]
}
